!!! 5
html
  head
    meta(http-equiv="content-type", content="text/html; charset=UTF-8")
    meta(charset="utf-8")
    link(rel="stylesheet", type="text/css", href="bootstrap-btn.css")
    style.

      /****************
       * THE DOCUMENT *
       ****************/
      
      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
        height: 100%;
      }
      
      body {
        font-family: "Lucida Sans Unicode", "Lucida Grande", sans-serif;
      }

      .contents {
        margin-top: 50px;
        margin-left: 50px;
        margin-right: 50px;
        max-width: 700px;
      }

      .lead {
        font-size: 200%;
        font-weight: 500;
      }
      .lead-big {
        font-size: 300%;
        text-transform: uppercase;
      }

      section {
        overflow: hidden;  /* make new box rendering dealy */
        margin-top: 60px;
      }

      header {
        font-size: 300%;
      }

      svg {
        margin-top: 20px;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
      }

      .btn-label {
        overflow: hidden;
        padding-left: 20px;
      }


      /***************
       * THE CATERER *
       ***************/

      .axis {
        shape-rendering: crispEdges;
      }

      .axis line {
        stroke: #fff;
      }

      .axis .minor {
        stroke-opacity: .5;
      }

      path.line {
        fill: none;
        stroke: #666;
        stroke-width: 1.5px;
      }

      .axis line, .axis path {
        fill: none;
        stroke: #000;
      }

      .row .divider {
        fill: none;
        stroke: #CCC;
      }
      
      .row .background {
        opacity: 0
      }

      .bar {
        fill: gray;
      }
      
      .bar.draggable {
        cursor: pointer;
      }

      .bar.draggable:hover {
        stroke: black;
      }

      .bar.drag-source {
        fill: lightgray;
      }

      .bar.fake-ok {
        fill: none;
        stroke: black;
        stroke-dasharray: 5,5;
      }

      .bar.fake-not-ok {
        fill: none;
        stroke: red;
        stroke-dasharray: 5,5;
      }

      .arrow-line {
        stroke: black;
      }
    
      /********************
       * THE CARTOGRAPHER *
       ********************/

      .region .fill {
        stroke: none;
        fill: white;
      }

      .borders {
        stroke: black;
        fill: none;
      }

      .palette .background {
        fill: lightgray;
      }

      
      .color-1 { color: #e41a1c; }
      .color-2 { color: #377eb8; }
      .color-3 { color: #4daf4a; }
      .color-4 { color: #984ea3; }
      .color-5 { color: #ff7f00; }


  body
    .contents
      .lead
        | Hello! I have
        .lead-big Two games
        | that I'd like you to play!
      p.
        (Depending on your attitude, they might not be "games" so much as "puzzles", or maybe "tedious bureaucratic exercises", but I <i>promise</i> I'm going somewhere with this.)
      section
        header #1: The Frugal Caterer
        p You run a successful catering service, dispatching teams to deliver, set up, and serve meals at events all across the city. You arrive at the office one morning, excited to see a full line-up of events happening throughout the day.
        #caterer-events
        p Now it's time to assign events to teams. Your teams work hard and move fast, but a team can't be in two places at once. So, given that constraint, your goal is to pull off the schedule using as few teams as possible. Hence your nickname &mdash; the Frugal Caterer. Get used to it.
        p Drag the events below to move them between teams. See if you can come up with the smallest possible number of teams.
        #caterer-dragging
        div
          .btn.btn-success.pull-left#caterer-done I'm all done!
          .btn-label#caterer-done-msg
      section
        header #2: The Frugal Cartographer
        p You are a master mapmaker, compiling the next great atlas of the world &mdash; continent by continent. First up: South America.
        p After a few months of work, you've come up with a killer outline. It looks like:
        #cartographer-outlines
        
        p It looks alright, but it's definitely missing something. <span class='color-1'>C</span><span class='color-2'>o</span><span class='color-3'>l</span><span class='color-4'>o</span><span class='color-5'>r</span>! Not only does color class up any drabby old atlas &mdash; it also makes it easier to tell countries apart as you read a map.
        p So your next step is color in the map.
        table
          tbody
            tr
              td(style='padding-right: 10px') You'd like to use as few different colors as possible, since that makes the map look prettier, and the printing cheaper. (You are the Frugal Cartographer, after all.)
              td(style='padding-left: 10px') But you still need to make sure that neighboring countries are always shaded with two different colors, or you'll confuse your readers and defeat the purpose of coloring in the first place.
            tr
              td
                #cartographer-howmany
              td(align='middle')
                img(src='bordering.png', width='159', height='150')
        p Use the palette below to color in the countries. See if you can color the whole map with the smallest possible number of colors.
        
        #cartographer-coloring  
        
        div
          .btn.btn-success.pull-left#cartographer-done I'm all done!
          .btn-label#cartographer-done-msg
        
      section
        header Common ground
        p As coy as I'm trying to be here, I don't think I'm fooling anyone. I wouldn't be bringing up these two games if there weren't some connection between them. You may have started noticing a connection yourself&hellip;
        p In both cases, you're trying to do something as frugally as possible, while following certain rules. And the rules turn out to be surprisingly parallel:
        ul
          li As the Frugal Caterer, <br> you assign <b class='color-1'>events</b> to <b class='color-2'>teams</b>, <br> making sure <b class='color-3'><b class='color-1'>events</b> which overlap in time</b> <br> are never placed in the same <b class='color-2'>team</b>.
          li As the Frugal Cartographer, <br> you paint <b class='color-1'>countries</b> with a set of <b class='color-2'>colors</b>, <br> making sure <b class='color-3'><b class='color-1'>countries</b> which border one another</b> <br> are never given the same  <b class='color-2'>color</b>.
        p I've colored in the parts which match up in the two descriptions.
        table
          tbody
            tr
              td(class='color-1')
                b The objects
              td events
              td countries
            tr
              td(class='color-2')
                b The groups
              td teams
              td colors
            tr
              td(class='color-3', style='width:200px')
                b Pairs of objects which cannot be grouped together
              td overlapping events
              td neighboring countries
      
      div(style='height:300px')

    script(src="react-with-addons-0.12.2.js")
    script(src="underscore.js")
    script(src="jquery.js")
    script(src="d3.js")
    script(src="utils.js")
    script(src="scheduler2.js")
    script(src="topojson.js")
    script(src='coloring2.js')
